after seven straight weeks of losses u.s  stock indexes ended one of the worst  sell-offs in history the s p 500 had its  worst 100 days to start a year since  1970 and the nasdaq had its worst first  100 days ever in the history of the  index while some are betting that the  worst is over i'm going to tell you why  there could be more selling to come  we'll break down how this sell-off  compares to past downturns provide some  insight on where things stand for the  u.s economy and detail why things are  likely uphill from here  let's get into it  there have been comparisons between this  sell-off and the dot-com bubble burst if  2022 is anything like those days there's  a lot further down to go  from the peak of the bubble in 2000 to  its trough in 2002 the nasdaq fell 78  at its lowest during this downturn the  nasdaq had fallen 32 from its most  recent high  further companies like tesla gamestop  and other tech favorites are still  showing incredibly high valuations  gamestop has a market cap of 10.5  billion dollars that's larger than  household appliance juggernaut whirlpool  and hyatt hotels tesla still has a  market cap of nearly 800 billion dollars  making it worth more than every other  car company in america combined  even though it sells less than five  percent of all cars in america so what's  the current state of the u.s economy  while the market downturn we've seen has  been significant businesses are holding  up well u.s corporate bankruptcies were  the lowest they've been during the first  quarter and at least 20 years  and the percentage of businesses that  are delinquent on their loans remains  historically low consumers are also  healthy according to credit agency  transunion which described american  consumers credit recently as strong  retail sales bank deposits and other  measures of consumer spending have also  held steady or increased so far in 2022  that all suggests the stock market's  losses have done limited damage to the  real economy that's a good thing but it  also means the stock market is unlikely  to get much help from the federal  reserve  in the near term the fed has only raised  u.s interest rates twice from nearly  zero percent  and when factoring in inflation rates  are still negative so throughout all the  turmoil in markets the fed had been  providing accommodative monetary policy  that's about to change  starting in june the fed will begin  what's known as quantitative tightening  meaning that rather than pumping money  into the economy they're sucking it out  and minutes from the fed's latest  meeting show the central bank is  considering moving towards restrictive  policy levels meaning raising interest  rates high enough that they restrict  economic growth that's something the u.s  hasn't seen in a generation and it could  put more pressure on stock prices but on  the bright side markets are  forward-looking so this may have already  been factored in and there's a chance  that the worst is behind us but there  are certainly some bumps up ahead  the legendary big daddy kane told us all  in 1992 i gotta take control of my own  destiny control what i hold and of  course be the boss of myself  no one else will bring my wealth  that's all for now i'll see you next  time  you
 

